  "text": "**Solution 3 (n/t) is wrong.** The Gibbs integration-by-parts recursion it uses is sound, but the result cannot hold. The curves are ordered, so H_{n+1} ≤ H_n and E[e^{H_{n+1}-H_n}] ≤ 1 for every n. The formula n/t exceeds 1 once n > t, so it is impossible. The problem's wording also implies a constant independent of n, t and x, which n/t is not.\n\n**Solution 1 (1) is wrong.** The gaps H_n − H_{n+1} are strictly positive almost surely, so e^{H_{n+1}-H_n} = e^{-gap} < 1 almost surely. Its expectation therefore cannot equal 1.\n\n**Solution 2 (1/2) is the only candidate not ruled out by these checks, but I could not verify it.** Its derivation is hand-waving: the \"Ward identity\" and \"energy sum rule\" are asserted, not derived. Its value does satisfy the constraints I could check: it lies in (0, 1), and it is independent of n, t and x as the problem implies. I could not confirm the constant itself, which depends on the Brownian bridge normalization and the exact parameterization of the KPZ line ensemble.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
